test_bufmgr - regression tests for the buffer manager
-----------------------------------------------------

This module tests the PostgreSQL buffer manager (src/backend/storage/buffer/)
as a subsystem, not any one replacement algorithm.  It is named for the
subsystem under test, which is stable, rather than the current eviction policy,
which is not: the replacement algorithm changes release over release.

We test properties that any correct buffer manager must have, expressed only through
observable behavior i.e. page residency, page contents, and buffer pins and
never through internal field encodings. These are meant to keep passing across
an algorithm swap: on today's usage-count clock sweep, or on a future pluggable
pool that may not even have a usagecount field.

test_bufmgr_eviction_reload    Under pressure whose working set exceeds
                               shared_buffers, some previously-resident
                               unpinned page is evicted, reloading it returns
                               correct contents, and the manager makes
                               progress (no "no unpinned buffers" error).

test_bufmgr_dirty_persistence  An evicted dirty page is written to disk, so a
                               reload returns the modified contents.

test_bufmgr_scan_no_retain     A page read once and never again does not
                               indefinitely retain its buffer under pressure.

test_bufmgr_truncate_stale     TRUNCATE leaves no stale cached pages that
                               later become visible.

test_bufmgr_drop_recreate_stale  A dropped relation's cached pages never
                               resurface through a newly created relation.

test_bufmgr_local_eviction     A temp relation larger than temp_buffers stays
                               correct after local-buffer reuse.

test_bufmgr_churn              Contents stay correct after many
                               replacement/reload cycles over a working set
                               much larger than the pool.

test_bufmgr_pinned_safety      (isolation) A pinned buffer is never evicted.
                               While one backend holds a cursor pin, another
                               floods past the whole pool; the pinned page
                               stays resident and unchanged while an unpinned
                               control block is evicted (proving the flood
                               really exceeded the pool).

Guiding tenet
-------------

Core tests asserts observable outcomes, not internal encodings.  Residency is read
as "is there a pg_buffercache row for this relation block", contents as the
page data itself, and pins as pg_buffercache.pinning_backends, never by
reading usagecount or any replacement-policy internal.

Uniquely identifiable pages: each row's payload is a deterministic function of
its id (md5(id::text)), so any block read back is verified by re-deriving the
expected payload, with no assumption about how many rows land on a page or
which page a row occupies so the tests are BLCKSZ independent.

Self-calibrating flood: eviction is forced not by a hand-tuned flood size but
by a bounded loop that grows a throwaway relation until the *observed* target
end-state is reached (a tracked block becomes non-resident), then stops so
the tests assert "reclaim was observed" rather than "N rows happened to be the
right number on this machine".  A generous round cap turns a real failure into
a prompt error instead of an infinite loop.  Where a fixed size is needed
instead of a loop (churn, local_eviction, the pinned-safety flood), it is
derived from the relevant GUC in *bytes* (shared_buffers or temp_buffers times
block_size), so the working set exceeds the pool at any BLCKSZ rather than at
one hand-tuned row count.

Ring-free pressure via pg_prewarm: the flood tests pull blocks into
shared_buffers with pg_prewarm in 'buffer' mode, which uses the normal
replacement path with NO BufferAccessStrategy ring so that warming of pages
is agnostic of ring buffer which is internal to buffer manager.

Configuration knobs
-------------------

test_bufmgr_small.conf:

shared_buffers = 8MB          1024 buffers - small enough that a modest flood
                              forces real reclaim through the whole pool, but
                              large enough that the test's own setup traffic
                              doesn't perturb tracked blocks before the flood.
temp_buffers = 100            Minimum local pool, so test_bufmgr_local_eviction
                              can exceed it. (Cannot change once a session has
                              touched a temp table, hence set in the config.)
bgwriter_lru_maxpages = 0     With it enabled, BgBufferSync's own demand-driven
                              LRU sweep runs concurrently with a test's flood
                              and reclaims buffers on its own schedule,
                              competing for the same clock-hand position -
                              test_bufmgr_pinned_safety's unpinned control
                              probe intermittently survived a flood that
                              should have evicted it (probe_evicted flipped
                              to false) because the bgwriter's independent
                              sweep had already changed which buffers were
                              reusable by the time the flood ran. Disabling
                              it removes that second, timing-dependent source
                              of reclaim so only the test's own flood moves
                              the tracked blocks.
autovacuum = off              Remove other background sources of buffer
                              touches during each test's brief window.
checkpoint_timeout = 1h       CHECKPOINT clears a buffer's dirty bit
                              (pg_buffercache.isdirty t -> f) WITHOUT evicting it,
                              the block stays resident. So a background checkpoint firing
                              mid-test would not itself cause a failure, but
                              it would silently do test_bufmgr_dirty_persistence's
                              write-out for it before the flood ever evicts
                              the block, the assertion would then pass because the checkpointer
                              wrote it, not because eviction of a dirty
                              buffer forces a write, which is the property actually under test.
client_min_messages = warning  Suppresses NOTICEs.

Follow-up work (TODOs)
----------------------
Deferred from this first iteration, roughly in the order they'd strengthen the
suite:
- Crash-durability of dirty pages across a real server restart (TAP test).
- Scan resistance test: a genuinely hot page survives a
  greater-than-pool one-touch flood.  Needs injection-point control over
  buffer placement (or a comparative hit-ratio measure) to be portable and
  policy-fair, rather than a single post-flood residency check.
- Background writer as a first-class subject. Its functionally-critical
  properties that it keeps clean, reusable victims available ahead of
  demand, and never writes data ahead of the WAL are timing-dependent or
  need fault injection, so they cannot be asserted deterministically in a
  plain regress test.
- Concurrent reads of the same relation block; only one physical read when
  multiple backends request the same uncached block.
- Cleanup-lock acquisition while other backends hold buffer pins.
- Asynchronous and streaming buffer reads.
- I/O failure handling and waiter wakeup; WAL-before-data ordering via fault
  injection.
- Checkpoint / buffer-writeback interactions; buffer statistics accounting.
- Behavior across different buffer access strategies (where applicable).
